WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.516
Maximum likelihood uses only the observation model. Bayesian modelling also describes plausible parameters before seeing the

00:00:08.516 --> 00:00:16.832
current observations. The posterior combines these sources; a maximum a posteriori estimate selects its highest-density parameter

00:00:16.832 --> 00:00:19.950
rather than reporting the whole distribution.

00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:27.187
The first term rewards matching the observation. The second charges for weight magnitude; its coefficient states

00:00:27.187 --> 00:00:28.027
the tradeoff.

00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:34.115
The penalty contributes to the derivative too. Minimizing only the data term would give a different

00:00:34.115 --> 00:00:34.463
answer.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:41.214
Will a tighter zero-centred prior move this estimate toward the observation or toward zero? Pause here

00:00:41.214 --> 00:00:43.557
and make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:51.610
Collecting the terms in w gives this solution. The positive curvature makes it the unique global

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:58.553
minimizer in this example. For our starting inputs, Unpenalized fit: 2. Regularized fit: 1. Data error:

00:00:58.553 --> 00:00:58.987
1.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:07.013
With observation variance one, multiplying the Gaussian negative log posterior by two produces the stated objective.

00:01:07.013 --> 00:01:10.770
A tighter zero-centred prior shrinks the MAP estimate.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:16.967
A MAP estimate is one point summary. It does not show posterior spread, and its numerical

00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:19.800
value depends on the stated prior and likelihood.

